   "about": "The development of Japan’s proletarian parties from the 1930s through their wartime dissolution and postwar revival.",
   "summary": "The report describes the proletarian parties’ electoral gains in 1936–37, the Shakai Taishutō’s shift toward support for Japan’s war in China, and the dissolution or suppression of independent left-wing organizations. It follows Japanese antiwar groups in China, emphasizing Nosaka Sanzō’s efforts to build a united-front organization among Japanese prisoners and exiles. Its postwar sections examine the Socialist Party’s factions, structure, constitutional proposals, and economic, labor, agricultural, foreign, and social policies. The final pages begin an account of the Communist Party’s revived leadership and the relationship between Tokuda and Nosaka.",
